Splunk SPL
/*
================================================================================
QUERY TITLE      : Authentication Anomaly & Brute Force Detection
FILE NAME        : authentication_anomalies.SPL
AUTHOR           : Serah Kathini Mulwa
TARGET PLATFORM  : Splunk Enterprise / Splunk Cloud
TARGET INDEX     : cybersecurity_lab
SOURCETYPE       : _json
DATE CREATED     : 2026-11-08
STATUS           : Tested & Portfolio Ready (Lab-Calibrated)
================================================================================

OBJECTIVE & ANALYTICAL INTENT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This threat-hunting SPL query scans normalized security telemetry to inspect
authentication failure volume, targeted accounts, and timing across sources
and hosts in the cybersecurity_lab index.

Note on field extraction and lab dataset calibration:
- In Splunk _json ingestion, nested JSON keys may appear as dotted fields or
  multi-value/array fields (such as "event.category{}"). Always verify field
  extraction in Splunk search before relying on a filter.
- The Day 22 lab sample (normalised_events.jsonl) contains 8 events total,
  including 2 failed authentication events from 192.0.2.25. A production
  threshold like > 3 filters out a 2-event lab sample, so this query defaults
  to total_failures >= 2 for lab testing and documents how to raise it for
  higher-volume hunting.

KEY FIELDS EVALUATED:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- index                 : Scopes search to the isolated lab index (cybersecurity_lab).
- sourcetype            : Targets structured JSON lab telemetry (_json).
- event.outcome         : Identifies authentication outcomes (e.g., "failure").
- event.action          : Identifies event actions (e.g., "authentication_failure").
- source.ip             : Originating IP address of the authentication attempt.
- user.name             : Targeted user account name.
- host.name             : Target host name when present in the event.
- _time                 : Splunk event timestamp (epoch seconds).

ANALYTICAL INTERPRETATION & SOC WORKFLOW:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Threshold Trigger   : Flags source IPs meeting total_failures >= 2 in the
                         lab sample (raise to > 3 or higher in broader hunts).
2. Cardinality Analysis: Calculates distinct targeted accounts via dc(user.name).
  - Elevated failures + 1 account  = Targeted brute-force or password attempt lead.
  - Elevated failures + N accounts = Potential multi-account spray/stuffing lead.
3. False Positive Check: Low failure counts or isolated typos require context
                         (successful logins, host logs, source context) rather
                         than assuming malicious intent.
================================================================================
*/

index=cybersecurity_lab sourcetype="_json" event.outcome="failure"
| stats
    count AS total_failures,
    dc(user.name) AS distinct_targeted_accounts,
    values(user.name) AS targeted_account_list,
    earliest(_time) AS first_epoch,
    latest(_time) AS last_epoch
    BY source.ip, host.name
| where total_failures >= 2
| eval first_observed = strftime(first_epoch, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z"),
       last_observed = strftime(last_epoch, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z"),
       duration_minutes = round((last_epoch - first_epoch) / 60, 2)
| sort - total_failures
| table source.ip, host.name, total_failures, distinct_targeted_accounts, targeted_acc
